WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.400
Movie Big Bang The Origins

00:02.400 --> 00:07.680
Imagine if we could rewind the clock, all the way back to the beginning of everything.

00:07.680 --> 00:09.920
What would we see?

00:09.920 --> 00:12.920
It's a question that tickles the mind, isn't it?

00:12.920 --> 00:19.000
A question that has led to one of the most intriguing theories of all time, the Big Bang.

00:19.000 --> 00:20.000
Picture this.

00:20.000 --> 00:25.720
The entire universe, all the stars, galaxies, and everything we know, sparked into existence

00:25.720 --> 00:27.640
from a singular event.

00:27.640 --> 00:31.400
It's like setting off the world's biggest firework, and we're still seeing the light

00:31.400 --> 00:33.680
show billions of years later.

00:33.680 --> 00:36.480
Now imagine scientists as cosmic detectives.

00:36.480 --> 00:41.160
They're on a mission, trying to piece together the clues left behind by this grand event.

00:41.160 --> 00:46.520
They scrutinize every cosmic nook and cranny, every whisper of light, every speck of cosmic

00:46.520 --> 00:47.600
dust.

00:47.600 --> 00:51.640
Like detectives following a trail, they're on the hunt for answers to explain our very

00:51.640 --> 00:53.640
existence.

00:53.640 --> 00:56.160
So how do we solve this cosmic mystery?

00:56.160 --> 01:00.240
Let's rewind the clock together and see what we find.

01:00.240 --> 01:03.640
The Big Bang Theory is not just an idea or a guess.

01:03.640 --> 01:09.000
It's a scientific explanation backed by a mountain of evidence.

01:09.000 --> 01:14.600
Imagine if you will, a time when everything that exists, every star, every galaxy, every

01:14.600 --> 01:20.280
grain of sand, was compressed into a single infinitesimally small point.

01:20.280 --> 01:23.300
This is the idea at the heart of the Big Bang Theory.

01:23.300 --> 01:29.060
It suggests that about 14 billion years ago, this tiny point began to expand rapidly, a

01:29.060 --> 01:31.620
process that continues even today.

01:31.620 --> 01:36.120
As the universe expanded, it cooled down, allowing particles to form, which eventually

01:36.120 --> 01:41.660
clumped together to create atoms, stars, galaxies, and everything we see around us.

01:41.660 --> 01:43.420
But how do we know this?

01:43.420 --> 01:44.420
What's the evidence?

01:44.420 --> 01:46.540
Well, there's quite a bit.

01:46.540 --> 01:51.580
For starters, we can see that galaxies are moving away from us in every direction, indicating

01:51.580 --> 01:54.680
the universe is still expanding.

01:54.680 --> 02:00.140
Then there's the Cosmic Microwave Background Radiation, a faint glow left over from the

02:00.140 --> 02:04.500
early universe that can be detected with special telescopes.

02:04.500 --> 02:11.260
This radiation is remarkably uniform across the sky, just as the Big Bang Theory predicts.

02:11.260 --> 02:16.940
And finally, the distribution of elements in the universe, like hydrogen and helium,

02:16.940 --> 02:21.060
aligns perfectly with what we'd expect from the Big Bang.

02:21.060 --> 02:26.100
The Big Bang Theory gives us a window into the past, but there's still so much more

02:26.100 --> 02:28.060
to discover.

02:28.060 --> 02:33.440
But even with the Big Bang Theory, there are still many mysteries left to solve.

02:33.440 --> 02:39.500
In our cosmic journey, we've come across phenomena that baffle even the brightest minds.

02:39.500 --> 02:41.780
Take dark matter, for instance.

02:41.780 --> 02:47.860
It's a substance that we can't see, can't touch, yet it makes up about 85% of the universe's

02:48.860 --> 02:52.980
And then there's dark energy, a force that's pushing galaxies away from each other at an

02:52.980 --> 02:54.460
accelerating pace.

02:54.460 --> 02:56.060
What's causing this?

02:56.060 --> 02:58.100
We're not quite sure yet.

02:58.100 --> 03:02.340
And let's not forget the ultimate mystery, the fate of the universe.

03:02.340 --> 03:07.500
Will it keep expanding forever, or will it one day collapse in a big crunch?

03:07.500 --> 03:12.340
These are questions that continue to perplex scientists and fuel ongoing research.

03:12.340 --> 03:16.660
So while we've made great strides in understanding our universe, there's still so much more

03:16.660 --> 03:18.700
to learn.

03:18.700 --> 03:23.520
The universe is full of mysteries, and we're just beginning to uncover its secrets.

03:23.520 --> 03:28.580
The story of the universe is a cosmic journey, and we're all part of it.

03:28.580 --> 03:32.700
We've traveled through the Big Bang's fiery beginning and pondered the unsolved enigmas

03:32.700 --> 03:34.460
that linger.

03:34.460 --> 03:38.780
This grand tale of cosmic proportions is not just out there in the far reaches of space,

03:38.780 --> 03:41.940
but right here, in us.

03:41.940 --> 03:46.940
We're made of star stuff, fellow travelers on this incredible voyage.

03:46.940 --> 03:52.620
So next time you look up at the stars, remember, you're not just looking at the universe,

03:52.620 --> 03:53.220
you're part of it.

